{
  "note": "The editor's sliders. `groups` is the page's layout and web/editor/regions.ts lists their order; `section` puts a caption above a slider in its group, `advanced` folds it away, and `measure` shows a card of the finished body's measurements under it. Read by web/editor/sliders.ts and sotaigen_dev/editor/sliders.py, which tests/test_editor.py keeps equal. File order is the order of application. What a group, section or slider is called, what a slider's ends mean and what its value means in the world and in which unit are messages of web/messages (group_<id>, section_<id>, slider_<name>, _low, _high, _help, _reading), in English and Chinese; `reading` turns a slider's value into another millimetre figure on the page, for what is still a control value or no length at all, see docs/PARAMETERS.md. A slider of an optional parameter starts where a body without it stands, its Param.default in sotaigen/params/spec.py. `torsoGains` add a share of the torso, for what is signed and so can't be scaled. `carriesShares` keeps every parameter measured in shares of the torso at its share as the torso changes, as sotaigen.params.tools.with_torso_length does. `brings` writes the parameters a slider needs beside it where the body has none, millimetres as shares of the torso. `lifts` raises a parameter in shares of the torso just enough to meet the rule it names (by its name in sotaigen/params/rules.py) after the slider moves, never lowering it: the thigh lifts the hip it can't keep shallow. Macro gains are a first guess, not looked at one by one. The head count's exponents are the table in docs/systems/params.md.",
